<!-- cammap - the feed: one entry per post on pages/blog.html.

     Written by hand. There is no build step and no generator on this
     site, and a page with one post does not need one. A new post on
     the Thoughts page is a new <entry> pasted under the ENTRIES line
     below, newest first, and the feed's own <updated> at the top set
     to that entry's date. The recipe, field by field:

       <title>      the post's heading, as text. Escape any ampersand
                    or angle bracket in it (&amp; &lt; &gt;).
       <id>         a tag URI, built from the post's id on blog.html:
                    tag:forrest404.github.io,YYYY-MM-DD:post-YYYY-MM-DD-title
                    It is an identifier, not an address, and it must
                    never change once published. A feed reader that
                    meets a new id has met a new post and tells every
                    subscriber again; a reader that meets the old id
                    with a new <updated> knows it is the same post,
                    edited. That is also why it is not the post's
                    URL: the site's address may move one day (NOTES.md,
                    "Sharing the site"), and the tag stays.
       <link>       the post's absolute address: the blog page plus a
                    hash and the article's id. This is what a reader
                    opens.
       <published>  the post's date, in RFC 3339 form -
       <updated>    YYYY-MM-DDT00:00:00Z - the same date the post's
                    <time datetime> carries, with a time of midnight
                    UTC because the post carries a day and not a
                    time, and an hour typed in would be a fact made
                    up. Both the same when a post is new; a later
                    edit to the post moves <updated> alone.
       <content>    the post's paragraphs, type="html", with every
                    left angle bracket and ampersand escaped (&lt;
                    &amp;) so that a reader shows the text itself and
                    not a summary of it. Curly quotes and dashes are
                    fine as they are; the file is UTF-8.

     Then the feed's <updated> becomes the newest entry's, so a reader
     polling the address sees at a glance that something changed.

     Atom rather than RSS: one format rather than two copies to keep
     in step; dates in one unambiguous form; and an author, an id and
     an updated required on the feed and on every entry, which is what
     lets the file be checked for shape without a network. The
     absolute addresses name the site's home on GitHub Pages; if it
     ever moves they change with every other file listed under
     "Sharing the site" in NOTES.md. The W3C validator at
     validator.w3.org/feed needs the deployed address, so it is a
     check to run after a push, not before.

     The author is the site and not a person. The site puts no names
     on itself, on principle, and the feed is not the place to start. -->
